insert into reading_passages (id, ielts_type, title, body, word_count, section_number) values
('20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001', 'academic', 'The Rise of Urban Beekeeping', 'Over the past two decades, a quiet agricultural movement has taken root on the rooftops and balconies of cities around the world: urban beekeeping. Once considered an eccentric hobby confined to rural smallholdings, beekeeping has migrated into dense metropolitan environments, with hives now found atop hotels, museums, and even government buildings in cities such as London, New York, and Paris.

The appeal of keeping bees in the city is, at first glance, counterintuitive. Cities are associated with concrete, pollution, and a scarcity of green space — hardly the idyllic meadows one might imagine as ideal habitat for honeybees. Yet research conducted over the last fifteen years has revealed a surprising pattern: urban bee colonies often outperform their rural counterparts in terms of both honey yield and colony survival rates. Several factors contribute to this phenomenon. Cities tend to be several degrees warmer than surrounding rural areas due to the "urban heat island" effect, extending the foraging season for bees by allowing flowers to bloom earlier in spring and later into autumn. Additionally, urban environments, paradoxically, often support a greater diversity of flowering plants than intensively farmed rural land, where monoculture crops and pesticide use can severely limit the variety and availability of nectar sources.

The growth of urban beekeeping has not been without controversy, however. Entomologists and conservationists have raised concerns that the trend, while well-intentioned, may inadvertently harm wild pollinator populations. Honeybees, it is argued, are farmed livestock rather than an endangered species, and a proliferation of managed hives in a limited urban area can create intense competition for the same finite pool of nectar and pollen. This competition disproportionately affects wild bee species — of which there are thousands, many solitary and far less visible than the honeybee — as well as other pollinating insects such as hoverflies and butterflies. A widely cited study conducted in London found a significant increase in the number of registered hives over a five-year period, far outpacing any corresponding growth in flowering plant coverage across the city, raising questions about the ecological carrying capacity of urban green space.

In response to these concerns, some cities have begun to regulate hive density, and a number of beekeeping associations now advocate for a more holistic approach: rather than simply adding more honeybee colonies, urban planners and citizens are encouraged to prioritise planting pollinator-friendly flowers, shrubs, and trees, and to reduce the use of pesticides in public parks and private gardens. Proponents argue that a city genuinely richer in floral resources would benefit not just honeybees but the full spectrum of native pollinators, many of which play a critical and underappreciated role in urban ecosystems.

Despite the debate over ecological impact, the cultural and educational value of urban beekeeping is rarely disputed. Rooftop apiaries have become valuable tools for environmental education, offering city dwellers, particularly children, a rare direct encounter with agricultural processes and the natural world. Numerous schools have installed observation hives, allowing students to watch bees at work through glass panels without risk of stings, fostering an early appreciation for the complex, cooperative societies that bees construct. Corporate sponsors, too, have embraced the trend, often as part of broader sustainability initiatives, installing hives on office rooftops and marketing the resulting honey as a locally sourced, environmentally conscious product.

Looking ahead, researchers suggest that the future of urban beekeeping will likely depend on cities adopting a more coordinated, data-driven approach — one that balances the genuine benefits of public engagement and education against the ecological costs of an unmanaged increase in honeybee density. Some cities have already begun mapping floral resources and hive locations to calculate a sustainable maximum number of colonies per square kilometre, a practice that may become standard as urban beekeeping continues to grow in popularity.', 430, 1),
('20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'general', 'Community Libraries in the Digital Age', 'When public libraries first proliferated in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, their core purpose was straightforward: to provide free access to books and information for people who could not otherwise afford them. Today, in an era when a vast amount of information is available instantly online, one might expect libraries to have declined in relevance. Instead, community libraries across many countries have reinvented themselves, evolving into multi-purpose civic spaces that serve functions far beyond book lending.

A visit to a modern community library might reveal a maker space equipped with 3D printers, a quiet study area used by students and remote workers alike, a children''s storytelling corner, and a bank of computers offering free internet access to those without a connection at home. Many libraries now also function as informal social service hubs, hosting job-seeking workshops, English language classes for new immigrants, and even partnerships with local health services to offer basic wellness checks. This transformation reflects a broader recognition that access to physical, non-commercial community space has itself become a scarce resource in many towns and cities, particularly as other traditionally free public venues have disappeared or become increasingly commercialised.

Library staff, whose roles were once centred on cataloguing and answering reference questions, have correspondingly taken on much broader responsibilities. Many librarians now receive training in areas such as digital literacy instruction, conflict resolution, and even, in some regions, basic mental health first aid, reflecting the reality that libraries are frequently the first point of contact for vulnerable community members, including those experiencing homelessness or social isolation.

Not everyone views this expanded role positively. Critics argue that libraries are being asked to fill gaps left by underfunded social services without a corresponding increase in library funding or staff training, placing an unsustainable burden on library employees who did not sign up for social work. Others worry that the traditional core function of libraries — quiet spaces for reading and reflection — is being crowded out by noise and activity associated with their new civic roles, alienating patrons who visit specifically for a calm environment.

Nonetheless, usage statistics from several countries suggest that far from becoming obsolete, community libraries are experiencing a resurgence in overall foot traffic, even as traditional book borrowing rates have gradually declined. This suggests that the value patrons place on libraries has shifted rather than disappeared: many now see the library primarily as a shared community space and a gateway to services, with book lending as one valuable offering among several. For local governments weighing budget priorities, this shift presents both an opportunity and a challenge — libraries may be more essential to community wellbeing than ever, but fulfilling that expanded mission convincingly will likely require funding models and staff training that go well beyond what nineteenth-century library founders could have anticipated.', 390, 1),
('21000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001', 'academic', 'The Return of the Night Sky', 'For most of human history, a clear night revealed thousands of stars overhead, a view so familiar that entire mythologies and navigation systems were built around it. Today, more than eighty per cent of the world''s population lives under skies affected by artificial light, and in heavily urbanised regions the Milky Way has become entirely invisible to the naked eye. This growing brightening of the night, known to scientists as skyglow, is now recognised as a distinct form of pollution with measurable effects on wildlife, human health, and scientific research, and a small but growing international movement is working to reverse it.

Skyglow occurs when artificial light is emitted directly into the sky or reflects off surfaces such as pavements and building facades, scattering through airborne particles and moisture to form a persistent glow above cities that can be seen from far beyond their boundaries. Satellite measurements taken over the past three decades show that the area of the Earth''s surface affected by artificial skyglow has expanded steadily each year, with the rate of increase in some regions outpacing population growth itself. This is partly attributed to the widespread adoption of light-emitting diode, or LED, street lighting, which is more energy-efficient than older sodium lamps but often emits a broader spectrum of light, including blue wavelengths that scatter more readily in the atmosphere and are known to interfere more strongly with biological processes.

The ecological consequences of this shift have become a major focus of recent research. Many species rely on natural light cycles to regulate behaviours such as migration, reproduction, and feeding. Sea turtle hatchlings, for instance, instinctively move toward the brightest visible horizon after emerging from their nests, a behaviour that for millions of years reliably guided them toward the reflective surface of the ocean. Coastal development and street lighting now frequently create a brighter artificial horizon inland, drawing hatchlings away from the sea and toward roads and buildings, where mortality rates are substantially higher. Similarly, nocturnal insects, many of which use moonlight for orientation, are drawn fatally toward artificial lights in what researchers describe as an ecological trap, and studies have linked declining insect populations in some regions partly to this disruption, alongside more widely discussed factors such as habitat loss and pesticide use.

Human health researchers have also raised concerns, focusing particularly on the disruption of circadian rhythms, the internal biological clock that regulates sleep, hormone release, and other physiological processes on a roughly twenty-four-hour cycle. Exposure to artificial light at night, especially light rich in blue wavelengths, has been shown to suppress the production of melatonin, a hormone that signals to the body that it is time to sleep. Long-term studies conducted in several countries have found associations between chronic exposure to bright nighttime environments and higher rates of sleep disorders, and some researchers have proposed links to broader metabolic and cardiovascular effects, although these associations remain an active area of investigation rather than settled fact.

Astronomers, meanwhile, were among the earliest professional groups to campaign against light pollution, for reasons that are more practical than ecological: observatories require genuinely dark skies to detect faint celestial objects, and skyglow can render certain observations impossible even with powerful telescopes. Several major observatories that once operated productively near urban centres have been forced to relocate to increasingly remote locations over the past century as nearby cities expanded, and some existing facilities now warn that continued growth in nearby artificial lighting threatens their long-term scientific usefulness.

In response to these accumulating concerns, a number of towns and cities around the world have adopted so-called dark-sky policies, which typically involve shielding street lights so that illumination is directed downward rather than outward and upward, replacing high-intensity blue-rich lighting with warmer-toned alternatives, and dimming or switching off non-essential lighting during late-night hours. Proponents argue that such measures do not require abandoning outdoor lighting altogether, but rather using it more deliberately: directing light only where it is needed, only as bright as necessary, and only for as long as required. Several jurisdictions that have implemented comprehensive dark-sky ordinances report that energy savings from more efficient, better-directed lighting have been substantial, offering a practical economic incentive that has helped such policies gain support even among officials who remain unpersuaded by ecological or astronomical arguments alone.

Despite this progress, sceptics note that dark-sky policies remain the exception rather than the rule, and that the global trend toward brighter, more extensively lit urban environments shows little sign of reversing on its own. Advocates counter that public awareness of light pollution has grown considerably in the past decade, pointing to a rising number of certified "dark-sky parks" and communities that actively market their star-filled skies as a tourist attraction, suggesting that what was once viewed purely as an unavoidable by-product of development is increasingly being recognised as a resource worth protecting in its own right.', 760, 1),
('21000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'academic', 'Ballast Water and the Silent Spread of Invasive Species', 'When a cargo ship is not carrying a full load of freight, it takes on seawater into large internal tanks to maintain its stability, structural balance, and manoeuvrability during the voyage. This water, known as ballast water, is drawn in at one port and typically discharged at another, often thousands of kilometres away, once cargo has been loaded and the additional weight of the ship''s cargo makes the extra water unnecessary. For over a century this practice was regarded as a purely mechanical necessity of maritime engineering, attracting little scientific attention. Since the 1980s, however, ballast water has come to be recognised as one of the most significant, and least visible, pathways by which marine species are transported across the globe and introduced into ecosystems where they do not naturally occur.

A single ballast tank can contain millions of litres of water drawn directly from a harbour or coastal area, along with whatever organisms happen to be present at the time: fish larvae, planktonic crustaceans, algae, bacteria, and the microscopic eggs and cysts of numerous marine species. When this water is later released at a different port, any organisms that survived the journey are introduced directly into a new marine environment, one that may lack the natural predators, parasites, or competitors that would normally keep such a population in check in its native range. Under the right conditions, a small number of introduced organisms can establish a breeding population and expand rapidly, sometimes with dramatic and costly ecological consequences.

Perhaps the most frequently cited example is the zebra mussel, a small freshwater mollusc native to lakes in Eastern Europe and Western Asia. Believed to have been introduced to the Great Lakes of North America via ballast water discharged from transatlantic cargo ships in the late 1980s, zebra mussels have since spread through interconnected waterways across much of the continent. Lacking natural predators in their new environment, they have reproduced at extraordinary densities, clogging water intake pipes at power plants and water treatment facilities, damaging boat engines, and outcompeting native mussel species for food and space. Economic estimates of the cumulative cost of managing zebra mussel infestations across North America run into billions of dollars, encompassing infrastructure repairs, water treatment modifications, and ongoing monitoring programmes.

Other documented cases illustrate the breadth of the problem beyond a single species or region. A species of comb jelly native to the western Atlantic Ocean was inadvertently introduced to the Black Sea in the 1980s, almost certainly via ballast water, where it multiplied at an astonishing rate in the absence of natural predators and consumed such vast quantities of plankton that it contributed to the collapse of several local fisheries that depended on the same food source. Similarly, certain toxic algal species capable of producing harmful blooms have been identified in ports far from their original range, arriving as dormant cysts within ballast sediment and later germinating under favourable local conditions.

Recognising the scale and economic cost of the problem, the International Maritime Organization adopted the Ballast Water Management Convention, an international treaty requiring ships to treat ballast water before discharge in order to kill or remove the vast majority of organisms it contains. Treatment methods vary and include filtration to physically remove larger organisms, ultraviolet irradiation to disrupt the reproductive capacity of smaller organisms, and the use of chemical disinfectants that are later neutralised before discharge. Since the treaty entered into force, newly built vessels have generally been required to install approved treatment systems, while older vessels have been granted phased compliance deadlines to allow shipping companies time to retrofit existing fleets, a process that has proved costly and logistically complex for an industry operating on narrow profit margins.

Enforcement of these requirements, however, remains uneven. Port authorities in wealthier nations with well-resourced maritime agencies have generally been able to inspect vessels and verify compliance relatively rigorously, whereas ports in some developing regions lack the equipment, trained personnel, or funding to test ballast water samples reliably, creating gaps through which non-compliant vessels may continue to operate largely unchecked. Some researchers have also raised concerns that certain approved treatment technologies, while effective against many organism types under laboratory conditions, may perform less consistently in the variable temperatures, salinities, and turbidity levels encountered in real-world ballast tanks, suggesting that ongoing monitoring and refinement of treatment standards will remain necessary even as compliance improves.

Looking beyond ballast water treatment alone, some marine biologists argue that a more comprehensive strategy is needed, one that also addresses other pathways of marine species introduction, such as organisms attached to a ship''s hull, a phenomenon known as biofouling, which some studies suggest may now rival ballast water as a source of new introductions. Regardless of the pathway, however, researchers broadly agree that the economic and ecological costs of established invasive marine species vastly exceed the cost of preventing their introduction in the first place, lending urgency to continued international cooperation on the issue.', 830, 2),
('21000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'academic', 'Cognitive Load Theory and the Design of Instruction', 'Since its formal introduction by the educational psychologist John Sweller in the 1980s, cognitive load theory has become one of the most extensively researched and widely applied frameworks in the science of learning. At its core, the theory begins with a distinction long recognised in cognitive psychology: the difference between working memory, the limited mental workspace in which information is actively held and manipulated, and long-term memory, an effectively unlimited store of organised knowledge that, once established, can be accessed with comparatively little conscious effort. Cognitive load theory is concerned specifically with how instructional design can either support or hinder the transfer of new information from the fragile, limited confines of working memory into the durable structures of long-term memory.

Central to the theory is the observation that working memory can hold only a small number of novel elements at once, typically estimated at somewhere between four and seven, before it becomes overloaded, and that any additional demand placed upon it beyond this capacity leads to a measurable decline in comprehension and retention. Sweller and subsequent researchers identified three distinct sources of this cognitive demand, or load. Intrinsic load arises from the inherent complexity of the material itself: understanding a straightforward historical date imposes little intrinsic load, whereas understanding the interacting causes of a complex historical event imposes considerably more, regardless of how well that material is taught. Extraneous load, by contrast, arises not from the material itself but from how it is presented, and is considered the primary target for improvement through better instructional design, since it represents cognitive effort that contributes nothing to learning. Germane load refers to the mental effort genuinely devoted to processing and integrating new information into existing knowledge structures, and is generally considered desirable, since it represents productive learning activity rather than wasted effort.

A substantial body of experimental research has identified specific instructional techniques that reduce extraneous load without compromising the material being taught. One of the most robust findings concerns what researchers term the "split-attention effect," which occurs when learners must mentally integrate two or more sources of information that are physically or temporally separated, such as a diagram on one page and its explanatory text on another, or a spoken narration that does not correspond in timing to an image on screen. Numerous studies have demonstrated that learners perform measurably better when related pieces of information are physically integrated, for instance by placing labels directly on the relevant part of a diagram rather than in a separate legend, since this arrangement removes the extraneous burden of searching for and mentally cross-referencing separated information.

A related and equally well-supported finding is the "redundancy effect," in which the presentation of the same information in two different but simultaneously redundant forms, such as identical text and narration presented at the same time, can paradoxically impair learning rather than reinforce it. The proposed explanation is that learners cannot fully ignore either channel of redundant information, and attempting to reconcile identical text and speech occupies working memory capacity that would otherwise be available for genuine comprehension, particularly for material of significant intrinsic complexity where spare working memory capacity is already limited.

Cognitive load theory has also generated a well-known and somewhat counterintuitive recommendation regarding worked examples, complete, fully solved illustrations of how to approach a particular type of problem. Contrary to an intuition that novices learn best through unguided problem-solving practice, controlled studies conducted primarily with learners new to a topic have repeatedly found that studying worked examples produces superior learning outcomes, at least in the early stages of skill acquisition, compared with attempting to solve equivalent problems from scratch. This has become known as the "worked example effect," and researchers explain it by noting that novice problem-solvers, lacking established mental structures for the domain, expend a disproportionate amount of working memory capacity on unproductive trial-and-error search strategies, leaving little capacity available for actually learning the underlying principles the exercise was meant to teach.

Notably, however, this advantage does not hold indefinitely as learners gain expertise. A substantial body of research has identified what is termed the "expertise reversal effect," whereby instructional techniques that benefit novices, including worked examples and highly explicit guidance, become progressively less helpful and can even become counterproductive once learners have developed a reasonable degree of proficiency in the domain. For more experienced learners, processing detailed step-by-step guidance that largely duplicates knowledge they already possess imposes its own form of extraneous load, and such learners generally benefit more from being given problems to solve with comparatively less scaffolding.

Critics of cognitive load theory, while generally not disputing its core experimental findings, have questioned whether the theory''s laboratory-derived principles translate straightforwardly into the complexity of real classrooms, where learners vary considerably in prior knowledge, motivation, and working memory capacity itself, and where a single lesson must often serve a highly heterogeneous group of students simultaneously. Some researchers have also noted that measuring cognitive load directly remains methodologically difficult, with most studies relying on indirect proxies such as self-reported effort ratings or performance outcomes rather than any direct physiological measurement, leaving open questions about precisely how the theory''s three proposed types of load can be reliably distinguished from one another in practice. Despite these open questions, the theory''s core recommendations, particularly regarding the integration of related information and the careful sequencing of worked examples and independent practice, continue to inform curriculum design and instructional materials across a wide range of educational contexts.', 900, 3),
('21000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'academic', 'The Fifteen-Minute City', 'Among the many ideas to have gained traction in urban planning circles over the past decade, few have attracted as much public attention, both enthusiastic and sceptical, as the concept of the "fifteen-minute city." First articulated in its modern form by the scientist and urbanist Carlos Moreno, the concept proposes that residents of a city should be able to meet most of their daily needs, including work, education, healthcare, groceries, and leisure, within a fifteen-minute walk or cycle of their home, reducing dependence on cars and long commutes while fostering a stronger sense of local community life.

The underlying idea is not entirely new. Urban theorists throughout the twentieth century repeatedly proposed variations on the notion of self-sufficient neighbourhoods, arguing that cities organised around car travel and rigid zoning, which separates residential, commercial, and industrial areas into distinct districts, tend to produce long, often stressful commutes and hollow out neighbourhood life outside of working hours. What distinguishes the fifteen-minute city as currently promoted is less the novelty of the underlying principle than the specificity of its proposed metric, and its explicit framing as a response to contemporary concerns including traffic congestion, air pollution, carbon emissions from transport, and, following widespread experience of local lockdowns during the recent global pandemic, a renewed public appreciation for neighbourhood-level amenities.

Implementing the concept in an existing city, as opposed to designing a new one from scratch, presents considerable practical challenges. Most cities that grew rapidly during the twentieth century did so around the assumption of widespread private car ownership, resulting in low-density suburbs where housing, employment, and services are often kilometres apart by design rather than accident. Retrofitting such areas to support a fifteen-minute lifestyle typically requires a combination of measures: relaxing zoning restrictions to permit small shops, clinics, and workspaces within residential areas; investing in pedestrian and cycling infrastructure; and encouraging, through incentives or regulation, a wider geographic distribution of amenities that might otherwise cluster only in a city''s commercial core. Several cities, including Paris and Melbourne, have adopted variations of the concept as explicit components of their long-term planning strategy, restructuring public investment and, in some cases, redesigning street layouts to prioritise pedestrians and cyclists over private vehicles.

Proponents argue that the potential benefits extend well beyond convenience. Reduced reliance on cars for short trips is associated with lower carbon emissions and improved local air quality, while increased walking and cycling offer public health benefits linked to higher levels of everyday physical activity. Advocates also point to social benefits, suggesting that neighbourhoods where residents routinely encounter one another while walking to local shops, schools, and parks tend to develop stronger informal social ties and a greater sense of collective responsibility for shared public spaces, in contrast to neighbourhoods that function largely as places to sleep between commutes elsewhere.

The concept has, however, attracted vocal criticism and, in some cases, considerable controversy. Some critics argue that the fifteen-minute city risks exacerbating existing inequalities: wealthier neighbourhoods, already well served by amenities, may see rapid improvement, while poorer or peripheral neighbourhoods, lacking the commercial investment or planning attention needed to attract new local services, may be left further behind unless deliberately prioritised for investment. Others raise a more pointed economic concern, noting that restricting car access in particular districts, a policy sometimes associated with fifteen-minute city initiatives, could disproportionately affect residents and small business owners who depend on vehicles for their livelihoods, such as tradespeople who transport tools and materials between job sites.

More surprisingly, in several cities the concept has become entangled in unrelated and largely unsubstantiated conspiracy theories, with some critics online falsely characterising traffic-reduction measures as an attempt to restrict residents'' freedom of movement rather than as a planning tool aimed at reducing unnecessary car trips. Urban planners and city officials involved in such initiatives have generally responded by emphasising that the fifteen-minute city is intended to add options, namely nearby amenities and safer streets for walking and cycling, rather than to remove existing ones, and that no city implementing the concept has proposed restricting residents from travelling wherever they wish by whatever means they choose.

Despite the controversy, empirical evaluation of fifteen-minute city initiatives remains at an early stage, since most implementations are recent and partial rather than comprehensive. Researchers studying early adopter cities note that meaningful assessment will require years of data on travel patterns, local business viability, and resident satisfaction across different neighbourhoods, cautioning against drawing firm conclusions, whether celebratory or critical, before such evidence has had time to accumulate.', 770, 1),
('21000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'academic', 'Mesopotamian Trade Networks and the Origins of Writing', 'For much of the twentieth century, the invention of writing in ancient Mesopotamia, in what is now southern Iraq, was popularly explained as an intellectual breakthrough driven primarily by religious or administrative record-keeping needs within temple institutions. While temples undoubtedly played a significant role in the development and spread of early writing, archaeological and textual evidence accumulated over the past several decades has increasingly pointed to a more specific and, in some ways, more mundane origin: the practical demands of long-distance trade and the accounting difficulties it created.

By roughly the fourth millennium BCE, the city-states of ancient Sumer had developed extensive trade networks reaching far beyond the fertile plains of the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, exchanging agricultural surplus, textiles, and manufactured goods for materials the region itself lacked, including timber, metals, and semi-precious stones sourced from distant regions to the north and east. Managing such trade at scale, particularly transactions involving goods that would be exchanged, transported, or stored over extended periods, created a persistent accounting problem: how could a merchant, temple administrator, or palace official reliably record, verify, and later recall the precise quantity and type of goods involved in a given transaction, especially once the transaction extended beyond the memory of any single individual present at the time?

The earliest known solution to this problem, predating writing by several millennia, involved small clay tokens of standardised shapes, each representing a specific commodity or quantity: a particular cone shape might represent a measure of grain, for instance, while a sphere might represent a larger quantity of the same. Archaeologists have recovered thousands of these tokens from sites across the ancient Near East, some dating back to as early as 8000 BCE, indicating a remarkably long period during which such tokens served as a functional, if limited, accounting system, likely used to track agricultural production, taxation, and the movement of goods within and between settlements.

As trade networks grew more complex, a further innovation emerged: tokens representing a particular transaction were sealed together inside a hollow clay ball, or "bulla," which could later be broken open to verify the contents against a physical delivery or claim. This system, however, presented an obvious inconvenience, since verifying the tokens sealed within a bulla required physically destroying it. The solution that followed proved pivotal: administrators began pressing the tokens into the still-soft exterior surface of the bulla before sealing them inside, creating a visible impression that recorded the container''s contents without requiring the ball to be broken open. This seemingly modest innovation represented a conceptual leap of considerable significance, since it separated the symbolic representation of a quantity from the physical tokens themselves; once the impressions alone were understood to convey the necessary information, the tokens inside the bulla became largely redundant.

From this point, the archaeological and textual record shows a gradual transition toward flat clay tablets bearing impressed or, increasingly, drawn symbols, marking the emergence of what scholars now call proto-cuneiform, an early pictographic script primarily concerned with the same accounting functions as its token predecessors: recording quantities of grain, livestock, textiles, and other traded goods, along with the names or titles of the individuals or institutions involved. Crucially, the earliest known texts of this kind are overwhelmingly administrative and economic in character rather than literary, religious, or historical, a finding that lends strong support to the trade-and-accounting theory of writing''s origin over earlier assumptions that writing began primarily as a vehicle for religious or literary expression.

Over subsequent centuries, this pictographic and increasingly abstract script evolved into the wedge-shaped cuneiform writing system, produced by pressing a reed stylus into soft clay tablets, which was eventually adapted to represent not just objects and quantities but the sounds of spoken language itself, enabling the recording of names, grammatical relationships, and, eventually, connected prose. This transition from a system limited to concrete accounting entries to one capable of representing the full complexity of spoken language is regarded by many historians of writing as one of the most consequential intellectual developments in human history, one that, on the available evidence, emerged not from a single moment of inspiration but from a slow, centuries-long process of incremental refinement driven substantially by the practical needs of merchants and administrators managing an increasingly complex trading economy.

Some scholars caution against overstating the purely economic origin of writing, noting that once a workable system of symbolic notation existed, it was rapidly adapted for other purposes, including royal inscriptions, legal codes, and religious hymns, and that later developments in writing''s expressive range owed as much to literary and political ambition as to accounting necessity. Nonetheless, the weight of archaeological evidence for the earliest phases of the system''s development, from clay tokens through sealed bullae to proto-cuneiform tablets, points overwhelmingly toward the practical challenges of long-distance trade as the original catalyst for one of humanity''s most transformative inventions.', 850, 2),
('21000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'academic', 'The Puzzle of Animal Self-Medication', 'For much of the history of modern biology, the deliberate use of medicinal substances was regarded as a distinctly human behaviour, dependent on cultural transmission, experimentation, and the accumulation of knowledge across generations. Over the past several decades, however, a growing body of field observations and controlled experiments has documented behaviours in a wide range of non-human animal species that appear, in function if not in underlying cognitive mechanism, remarkably similar to medicinal self-treatment. This field of study, now generally referred to as zoopharmacognosy, a term combining roots meaning "animal," "drug," and "knowing," has prompted a substantial rethinking of where the boundary between instinctive behaviour and adaptive, medicinally purposeful action truly lies.

Some of the earliest and most widely cited observations concern great apes. Field researchers studying chimpanzees in East Africa noted that individuals suffering from apparent intestinal parasite infections would occasionally seek out and chew the pith of a particular plant not otherwise part of their typical diet, swallowing the bitter juice while spitting out the fibrous remainder, a preparation method notably similar to that used by some local human populations for the same plant to treat intestinal complaints. Subsequent laboratory analysis confirmed that the plant contains compounds with demonstrated activity against intestinal parasites, lending strong support to the interpretation that the chimpanzees'' unusual feeding behaviour served a genuinely medicinal function rather than representing an incidental dietary preference.

A separate and mechanistically distinct example involves leaf-swallowing behaviour observed in several great ape species, in which whole, rough-surfaced leaves are swallowed without chewing, folded in a particular manner, and pass through the digestive tract largely intact. Researchers have proposed that the physical texture of these leaves helps to mechanically dislodge and expel intestinal parasites as they pass through the gut, a hypothesis supported by the observation that such behaviour occurs disproportionately during periods and seasons associated with elevated parasite loads, and that parasite egg counts in faecal samples often decline noticeably following episodes of leaf-swallowing.

Beyond primates, self-medicating behaviours have been documented across a striking range of species. Certain species of ants, upon sustaining injuries or fungal infection within the colony, have been observed to seek out and apply plant resins with antimicrobial properties to the nest structure itself, a behaviour that appears to reduce fungal growth and disease transmission among the closely packed colony members. Some birds line their nests with specific aromatic plants shown in laboratory testing to repel parasitic mites, a behaviour that varies measurably with local parasite pressure, being more pronounced in nesting sites and seasons where mite infestation is more severe. Perhaps most strikingly, certain species of butterfly appear to select host plants for their eggs based partly on the plant''s chemical profile, effectively provisioning their future offspring with compounds that will later confer some protection against parasites the larvae are likely to encounter, a behaviour some researchers describe as a form of medication administered not to the animal exhibiting the behaviour but to its future offspring.

These wide-ranging observations raise a genuinely difficult scientific question: to what extent do such behaviours reflect anything resembling deliberate, learned therapeutic action, as opposed to fixed instinctive responses shaped over evolutionary time by natural selection, requiring no understanding whatsoever on the part of the individual animal of why the behaviour is beneficial? For many of the invertebrate examples, most researchers favour a largely instinctive explanation, arguing that a behaviour conferring even a small survival or reproductive advantage could become genetically fixed within a population over many generations without requiring any cognitive understanding of cause and effect on the part of the individual animals performing it.

For some primate behaviours, however, the picture appears more complicated. Researchers have observed that not all individuals within a group engage in a given self-medicating behaviour at any one time, that the behaviour is sometimes observed to spread through a group in a pattern consistent with social learning, in which younger or less experienced individuals appear to acquire the behaviour partly by observing others, and that individuals sometimes appear to modify their behaviour in ways that suggest at least some responsiveness to their own apparent physical condition rather than a wholly fixed, automatic response triggered by an external cue alone. Such observations have led some researchers to argue for a form of "cultural" transmission operating alongside, or on top of, an underlying instinctive foundation, though this interpretation remains genuinely contested within the field, and demonstrating social learning conclusively in wild populations, as opposed to instinctive responses that merely happen to correlate with social proximity, remains notoriously difficult.

Beyond its intrinsic scientific interest, zoopharmacognosy has attracted growing attention from pharmaceutical researchers and ethnobotanists, on the reasoning that plants and other substances repeatedly selected by animals for apparent medicinal purposes across independent observations may represent a promising, if unconventional, starting point for identifying compounds of potential therapeutic value to humans, a research strategy sometimes informally described as "following the animals" to shortcut the traditionally slow process of screening plant compounds for medicinal potential. While this approach remains a relatively minor strand within pharmaceutical research compared with more conventional screening methods, several researchers argue that as instances of documented animal self-medication continue to accumulate across an increasingly diverse range of species, the practice deserves more systematic attention than it has historically received, both for what it might reveal about the evolutionary origins of medicinal behaviour and for its potential, however modest, to inform the search for new therapeutic compounds.', 920, 3),
('22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001', 'general', 'Riverside Fitness & Aquatic Centre: Member Handbook', 'Welcome to Riverside Fitness & Aquatic Centre, the newest leisure facility in Overton Borough. This handbook explains our membership options, opening hours, and the rules that apply to all members and their guests. Please keep a copy for reference, as it forms part of your membership agreement.

Opening Hours
The centre is open from 6:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m. Monday to Friday, and from 7:00 a.m. to 8:00 p.m. on Saturdays and Sundays. The swimming pool closes thirty minutes before the rest of the centre to allow time for cleaning. On public holidays the centre operates reduced hours, from 9:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m., and this schedule is posted at reception two weeks in advance.

Membership Types
We offer three membership categories. The Bronze membership, priced at £28 per month, grants access to the gymnasium and the group exercise studio only. The Silver membership, at £42 per month, includes everything in Bronze plus unlimited use of the swimming pool and sauna. The Gold membership, at £59 per month, includes everything in Silver plus two free personal training sessions each month and priority booking for the group exercise studio, which can otherwise fill up quickly during peak evening hours. All memberships require a minimum commitment of one month, and new members must complete a short induction session with a member of staff before using the gymnasium equipment for the first time.

Guest Policy
Members may bring up to two guests per visit, provided the guest is signed in at reception and accompanied at all times. Guests are charged a flat fee of £8 per visit, payable at reception, and may not bring guests of their own. Children under fourteen are not permitted in the gymnasium at any time, though those aged eight and above may use the swimming pool if accompanied by a paying adult member.

Cancellations and Refunds
Membership may be cancelled at any time after the first month by giving thirty days written notice to reception or by email to the membership office. Refunds are not offered for partial months. Members who cancel and later wish to rejoin within six months will be charged the standard £15 joining fee again, whereas members who rejoin after more than six months are exempt from this fee as a welcome-back incentive.

Locker Rooms and Lost Property
Lockers are available on a daily-use basis only; members should bring their own padlock, as any lock left overnight without prior arrangement will be cut off and its contents moved to lost property. Lost property is held for twenty-eight days before being donated to a local charity.', 420, 1),
('22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'general', 'Flexible Working and Leave Policy at Meridian Logistics', 'Meridian Logistics is committed to supporting staff in balancing their professional responsibilities with personal commitments. This section of the staff handbook outlines the company''s flexible working arrangements, annual leave entitlements, and the procedure for requesting time off. All permanent employees, regardless of department, are covered by the policies described below; agency and temporary staff should instead consult the separate Temporary Staff Handbook.

Flexible Working Arrangements
Employees who have completed at least six months of continuous service may apply for flexible working, which includes options such as compressed hours, staggered start and finish times, and, for roles that do not require a physical presence in the warehouse, partial remote working. Applications must be submitted in writing to a direct line manager using the Flexible Working Request Form, available from the intranet. Line managers are required to respond within twenty-one days, either approving the request, proposing a modified arrangement, or explaining in writing why the request cannot be accommodated at that time. Employees whose initial request is declined may appeal to the Human Resources department within fourteen days of receiving the decision.

Annual Leave
Full-time employees accrue twenty-five days of paid annual leave per calendar year, in addition to statutory public holidays. Part-time employees accrue leave on a pro-rata basis according to their contracted hours. Leave requests should be submitted through the online scheduling system at least two weeks in advance wherever possible, and no more than ten consecutive working days may be taken without prior written approval from a departmental head. Unused leave may be carried over into the following year only up to a maximum of five days, and any leave beyond this limit is forfeited unless the employee was unable to take it due to approved sick leave or maternity or paternity leave.

Sick Leave and Return-to-Work Procedure
Employees who are unable to attend work due to illness must notify their line manager by telephone before their scheduled start time, rather than by text message or email. For absences lasting more than seven consecutive calendar days, a medical certificate from a general practitioner is required. Upon returning to work after any period of sickness absence, employees are required to complete a brief return-to-work interview with their line manager, the purpose of which is to record the reason for absence, confirm the employee''s fitness to resume duties, and identify whether any workplace adjustments would help prevent similar absences in future.

Parental Leave
Employees expecting to take maternity, paternity, adoption, or shared parental leave should inform their line manager and the Human Resources department as early as reasonably possible, and no later than fifteen weeks before the expected date, to allow sufficient time for planning cover arrangements. The company offers an enhanced parental pay scheme for employees with more than two years of continuous service at the time leave commences, details of which are set out in a separate policy document available from Human Resources.

Training and Professional Development Scheme
All employees are entitled to apply for the Meridian Development Fund, which reimburses up to £600 per year towards approved external courses, professional qualifications, or conference attendance directly relevant to the employee''s current role or a realistic future role within the company. Applications must be approved in advance by a line manager and countersigned by a departmental head before any course is booked; retrospective claims for courses already completed will not be reimbursed under any circumstances.', 500, 2),
('22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'general', 'The Quiet Revival of Handwriting', 'For much of the twenty-first century, commentators have confidently predicted the demise of handwriting. As keyboards, touchscreens, and voice-to-text software became embedded in everyday communication, cursive script in particular came to be seen by many educators as a quaint relic, worth teaching only for its ceremonial use in signatures. Yet in the past several years, sales figures from stationery retailers, the popularity of journaling communities online, and a wave of academic research have combined to produce something few would have predicted a decade ago: a modest but measurable revival of interest in writing by hand.

Much of this renewed interest has been driven by psychological research comparing note-taking on laptops with note-taking by hand. In a series of widely discussed studies beginning in the mid-2010s, researchers found that students who took lecture notes with pen and paper performed better on conceptual questions afterward than students who typed their notes, even though the typists typically recorded more words overall. The explanation offered was not that typing itself impairs learning, but that the relative slowness of handwriting forces the note-taker to summarise and paraphrase material in real time rather than transcribing it verbatim, a process researchers term the "generation effect": information that must be actively reformulated, rather than merely copied, is more likely to be encoded in long-term memory.

Subsequent research using brain imaging has offered a possible neurological basis for these findings. Handwriting appears to activate a broader and more interconnected network of brain regions than typing, including areas associated with fine motor control, visual perception, and language processing. One proposed explanation is that forming each letter by hand requires a distinct, non-repeating sequence of movements, whereas typing involves the same small, repetitive motion regardless of which letter is pressed. Some researchers speculate that this richer sensorimotor engagement is what allows handwritten material to be recalled and recognised more readily, though they are careful to note that the studies conducted so far involve modest sample sizes and cannot fully rule out other explanations, such as differences in attention or motivation between the groups studied.

The educational implications of this research have proved contentious. Several school systems that removed cursive handwriting instruction from their curricula in the 2010s, judging it an inefficient use of classroom time in a digital era, have in recent years begun to reinstate it, citing both the cognitive research described above and a separate, more practical concern: students who are never taught cursive struggle to read historical documents, handwritten family letters, and even the cursive signatures required on many official forms. Critics of this reversal argue that reinstating cursive risks displacing instructional time that could be spent on typing proficiency and digital literacy, skills that are unambiguously necessary for most contemporary careers, whereas the practical need for cursive reading, they contend, could be addressed far more efficiently through a short, targeted unit rather than years of handwriting drills.

Outside the classroom, a parallel revival has taken hold among adults, many of whom did not grow up with any expectation that pen and paper would disappear from professional life. Publishers of notebooks and diaries report sustained growth in sales even among younger, digitally native consumers, a trend widely attributed to the popularity of "bullet journaling" and other structured, handwritten planning systems that blend note-taking with personal reflection. Advocates describe the slower pace of handwriting as a deliberate counterweight to what some have termed "digital fatigue": the sense of mental depletion that can follow long stretches of screen-based work and communication. For these users, the appeal of a notebook has little to do with efficiency and everything to do with the experience of writing itself, an activity many describe as calming in a way that typing rarely is.

Not everyone is persuaded that the evidence justifies the enthusiasm. Sceptics point out that the studies most frequently cited in popular discussions of handwriting typically measure performance on short-term recall tasks under controlled laboratory conditions, and that it remains unclear whether the same advantages would hold for the kind of sustained, complex work most people actually do in their studies or careers. Others raise a more practical objection: for individuals with dysgraphia, motor impairments, or certain learning differences, handwriting is not a superior option but a genuine barrier, and framing it as inherently preferable risks disadvantaging exactly the students who benefit most from typing and assistive technology. Romanticising a return to the past, these critics warn, should not come at the expense of tools that have made education more accessible to a wider range of learners than at any previous point in history.

Faced with these competing considerations, most researchers in the field stop well short of recommending a wholesale return to handwriting. Instead, the emerging consensus favours a more selective approach: recognising that handwriting appears to offer genuine benefits for certain kinds of learning, particularly tasks that require synthesising or reflecting on new material, while accepting that typing remains indispensable for speed, accessibility, and most professional communication. Several major note-taking application developers appear to have reached a similar conclusion, having introduced stylus-based handwriting recognition features that convert handwritten notes into searchable digital text. Rather than one technology displacing the other, then, the more likely future is a hybrid one, in which the choice between pen and keyboard depends less on habit or nostalgia than on the specific cognitive task at hand.', 830, 3),
('22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'general', 'Willowbrook Holiday Park: Guest Information and House Rules', 'Welcome to Willowbrook Holiday Park. We hope you enjoy your stay in one of our forty-two self-catering lodges, set within twelve acres of woodland beside the River Calder. Please read the following information carefully, as it forms part of the booking conditions you agreed to when reserving your lodge.

Check-in and Check-out
Check-in is from 3:00 p.m. on the day of arrival; lodges cannot be accessed before this time under any circumstances, as our housekeeping team requires the full day to prepare each lodge between guests. Check-out is by 10:00 a.m. on the day of departure. Guests requiring a later check-out should ask at reception at least twenty-four hours in advance; a late check-out until 12 noon can be arranged for a fee of £15, subject to availability.

Parking and Vehicles
Each lodge is allocated one parking space directly outside the property. Guests bringing a second vehicle must park in the overflow car park near the entrance and should register the vehicle''s registration number at reception on arrival. The speed limit throughout the park is 10 miles per hour at all times, strictly enforced to protect the many families with young children who walk freely around the site.

Pets
Well-behaved dogs are welcome in designated pet-friendly lodges only, of which there are eighteen across the park; a supplement of £25 per stay applies per dog, with a maximum of two dogs per lodge. Dogs must be kept on a lead at all times outside the lodge and are not permitted inside the swimming pool building, the restaurant, or the children''s play barn. Owners must clean up after their pets immediately; failure to do so may result in the loss of the deposit.

Fires and Barbecues
Open fires are not permitted anywhere on the park. Barbecues may be used only on the paved area immediately outside each lodge, using the barbecue provided, and must never be left unattended while lit. During periods of high fire risk, which are announced on the park noticeboard and website, barbecue use may be temporarily suspended for all guests.

Noise and Quiet Hours
To ensure all guests enjoy a peaceful stay, quiet hours apply between 11:00 p.m. and 8:00 a.m., during which amplified music and loud outdoor activity are not permitted. Guests who repeatedly breach quiet hours may be asked to leave without a refund.

Damage and Deposits
A refundable security deposit of £100 is taken at booking and returned within seven working days of departure, provided no damage or excessive cleaning is required.', 430, 1),
('22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'general', 'Warehouse Safety Induction Handbook: Cedar Distribution', 'All new employees and agency staff working within Cedar Distribution''s warehouse facilities must complete this safety induction before beginning any warehouse-based duties, regardless of previous experience gained elsewhere. This handbook summarises the key safety procedures; a full copy of the Site Safety Manual is available from the Health and Safety Officer and should be consulted for any procedure not covered here.

Personal Protective Equipment
High-visibility vests, safety footwear with reinforced toecaps, and hearing protection in designated high-noise zones are mandatory at all times within the warehouse floor, without exception. Hard hats are required only in areas marked with yellow-and-black signage, typically close to the racking bays where stock is loaded and unloaded above head height. Staff who repeatedly fail to wear required protective equipment will be removed from warehouse duties pending a formal review with their supervisor.

Forklift and Machinery Operation
Only employees holding a current, site-specific forklift licence, issued after completing Cedar Distribution''s internal training course, may operate powered industrial trucks. This licence must be renewed every three years through a half-day refresher course. Pedestrians must always give way to moving forklifts and should never walk through a racking aisle while a forklift''s amber warning light is flashing. Any employee who witnesses a near-miss involving a forklift, whether or not anyone was injured, is required to report it using the Incident Report Form within twenty-four hours.

Manual Handling
Employees expected to lift loads regularly as part of their role must complete a manual handling training session before starting work, and again every two years thereafter as a refresher. As a general guide, no individual should attempt to lift an item weighing more than 25 kilograms without mechanical assistance or the help of a second person; heavier items must be moved using a trolley, pallet truck, or forklift. Employees who experience back pain or discomfort after lifting should report this to their supervisor immediately rather than continuing to work through it.

Fire Safety and Emergency Procedures
Fire extinguishers are located at intervals throughout the warehouse and are colour-coded according to the type of fire they are designed to tackle; staff are not expected to memorise these codes but should never attempt to fight a fire that is spreading beyond its point of origin. In the event of a fire alarm, all staff must proceed immediately to their designated assembly point in the staff car park and must not re-enter the building until a manager confirms it is safe to do so. Fire drills are conducted twice per year, and attendance is compulsory for all staff on shift at the time.

Reporting Hazards
Any employee who identifies a potential hazard, such as a spillage, damaged racking, or blocked fire exit, should report it immediately to a supervisor and, where safe to do so, place a warning cone or sign near the hazard while awaiting a permanent fix. Hazards should also be logged in the Hazard Register kept at the site office, which is reviewed weekly by the Health and Safety Officer.', 480, 2),
('22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'general', 'The Science of Déjà Vu', 'Almost everyone has, at some point, experienced the peculiar sensation of feeling that an entirely new situation has somehow already happened before. This phenomenon, known as déjà vu — from the French for "already seen" — is remarkably common: survey estimates suggest that between sixty and seventy per cent of people experience it at least once in their lives, most commonly in early adulthood. For centuries, the sensation was regarded as unexplainable, even attributed by some to psychic premonition or evidence of a former life. Only in the last few decades has déjà vu become the subject of serious, systematic scientific investigation, aided by new techniques that allow researchers to study memory processes with far greater precision than was previously possible.

One of the leading explanations centres on what psychologists call "familiarity without recollection." According to this account, the brain has a rapid, largely automatic system for judging whether a scene, object, or situation matches something encountered before, based on overlapping features rather than the full sensory detail of the original memory. If a new environment happens to share enough incidental features with a genuinely forgotten past experience — a similar arrangement of furniture in an unfamiliar room, for instance — this rapid-matching system may trigger a strong sense of familiarity even though the individual cannot consciously recall when or where the earlier experience occurred. Researchers using virtual reality have tested this idea directly, constructing scenes that shared a spatial layout with an earlier scene participants had viewed but did not consciously notice; a meaningful proportion of participants reported a déjà-vu-like sensation when later placed in the superficially different but spatially similar scene.

A second, complementary line of evidence comes from clinical neurology. Patients with temporal lobe epilepsy frequently report intense and unusually frequent episodes of déjà vu in the moments immediately preceding a seizure, a phenomenon long noted by neurologists but poorly understood until recently. During pre-surgical evaluation, when doctors sometimes apply mild electrical stimulation directly to brain tissue in order to map the source of a patient''s seizures, stimulating structures in and around the temporal lobe — particularly a region known as the rhinal cortex — has in some cases reliably reproduced the sensation of déjà vu in fully conscious patients. This has led many researchers to conclude that déjà vu results not from an actual, if buried, memory being retrieved, but from a kind of false alarm: a brief misfiring of the neural circuitry responsible for signalling that something is familiar, occurring independently of whether it genuinely is.

These findings fit neatly within a broader, well-established framework in memory research known as the dual-process model, which distinguishes between two separate cognitive operations involved in recognising something: familiarity, a fast and relatively automatic gut feeling that something has been encountered before, and recollection, a slower and more effortful process that retrieves specific contextual details, such as when and where the earlier encounter took place. Under normal circumstances, these two processes work in tandem and tend to agree with one another. Déjà vu, in this framework, may represent a rare instance in which the familiarity system fires with unusual strength while the recollection system finds nothing to retrieve, producing the distinctive and slightly unsettling combination of certainty and emptiness that people describe: a powerful conviction of having been here before, with absolutely no memory of when.

Curiously, the frequency of déjà vu is not constant across the lifespan. Large surveys consistently find that the experience is most common among people between roughly fifteen and twenty-five years of age and becomes progressively rarer, and typically less intense, from middle age onward, a pattern that holds across most populations studied. Researchers have also found correlations between déjà vu frequency and factors such as tiredness, elevated stress, and frequent travel to unfamiliar places, though correlation alone cannot establish which, if any, of these factors is directly responsible. Some researchers argue that the decline with age reflects a genuine change in the efficiency or sensitivity of the brain''s memory-checking circuitry over time; others propose a simpler explanation, namely that younger adults, particularly those relocating, studying, or travelling extensively, simply encounter a far greater volume of novel-but-similar environments in which the familiarity system has an opportunity to misfire.

For much of the twentieth century, research into déjà vu was hampered by an obvious practical problem: the experience is brief, unpredictable, and cannot be summoned on demand, leaving researchers reliant on retrospective self-report long after the fact. This began to change with the development of laboratory methods capable of reliably inducing a déjà-vu-like state under controlled conditions, including the virtual reality technique described earlier as well as certain hypnosis-based suggestion procedures. While these lab-induced experiences may not be identical in every respect to spontaneous, naturally occurring déjà vu, they share enough of its defining features — a strong sense of familiarity combined with an inability to place its source — to give researchers, for the first time, a genuinely experimental method for studying the phenomenon, rather than relying solely on memory and anecdote.

Though fleeting and, for most people, entirely harmless, déjà vu offers researchers something unusually valuable: a naturally occurring window into how the healthy brain distinguishes genuine memory from mere perception, and what happens in the rare moments when that distinction briefly breaks down. Some researchers believe that understanding the mechanics of this everyday misfiring could eventually inform the study of more serious and persistent memory disturbances, including rare conditions in which patients experience chronic, near-constant feelings of déjà vu that interfere significantly with daily life. If the checking mechanism that ordinarily prevents false familiarity can be better understood in its mildest, most common form, researchers suggest, it may eventually help clinicians recognise and treat the far rarer cases in which that same mechanism fails in a much more serious and sustained way.', 850, 3);

