('reading', 'short_answer', 'Science', 'medium', 6, 'What term do psychologists use for the theory that a rapid brain system judges familiarity based on overlapping features?', '22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', null, null, '["familiarity without recollection","\"familiarity without recollection\""]', 'The passage introduces the term directly: "One of the leading explanations centres on what psychologists call familiarity without recollection."', 'Terms placed in quotation marks in academic-style passages are usually the exact answer expected for a short-answer question about terminology.', '{"short_answer"}', 55, 7, false),
('reading', 'multiple_choice', 'Science', 'medium', 6, 'According to the passage, what happens when doctors electrically stimulate the rhinal cortex in some epilepsy patients?', '22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', null, '["Patients lose consciousness immediately","Patients often report a sensation of déjà vu","Patients experience improved long-term memory","Patients report a decrease in seizure frequency"]', '"Patients often report a sensation of déjà vu"', 'The passage states stimulating the rhinal cortex "has in some cases reliably reproduced the sensation of déjà vu in fully conscious patients," which also rules out loss of consciousness.', 'Note the passage says patients remain "fully conscious" during stimulation, which directly eliminates the option about losing consciousness.', '{"multiple_choice"}', 65, 8, false),
('reading', 'true_false_not_given', 'Science', 'hard', 6.5, 'Doctors are certain that déjà vu is caused by an actual memory being retrieved from long ago.', '22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', null, null, '"FALSE"', 'The passage states déjà vu "results not from an actual, if buried, memory being retrieved, but from a kind of false alarm," which contradicts the statement.', 'The structure "not from X, but from Y" clearly rules out X as the accepted explanation, so treat X-based statements as false.', '{"true_false_not_given"}', 60, 9, false),
